NAME
       sysctl - show and change kernel settings at run time

SYNOPSIS
       sysctl [-n] NAME...
       sysctl [-w] NAME=VALUE...
       sysctl -a
       sysctl -p [FILE]
       sysctl --system

DESCRIPTION
       The kernel has hundreds of settings (kernel parameters) that can be
       changed while the system runs: whether it forwards network packets
       between interfaces, how eagerly it uses swap, how many files may be
       open. They appear as files under /proc/sys; the name
       net.ipv4.ip_forward is the file /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward.

       sysctl NAME prints a setting; sysctl NAME=VALUE (or -w) changes it at
       once. Such a change lasts only until the next reboot.

       To keep a setting, write NAME = VALUE in a file in /etc/sysctl.d whose
       name ends in .conf (for example /etc/sysctl.d/90-lab.conf). Those
       files are applied at every boot; sysctl --system applies them now.
       Lines starting with # or ; are comments.

OPTIONS
       -n, --values
              Print only the value, without the name. Good for tests.

       -w, --write
              Change the settings given as NAME=VALUE.

       -a, --all
              Print every setting and its value.

       -p, --load[=FILE]
              Apply the settings in FILE (by default /etc/sysctl.conf).

       --system
              Apply the settings from all the system's configuration
              directories: /usr/lib/sysctl.d, /run/sysctl.d, /etc/sysctl.d and
              finally /etc/sysctl.conf.

       -q, --quiet
              Do not print the values that were set.

EXAMPLES
       Show whether the system forwards IPv4 packets:

           sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward

       Make the kernel less eager to swap, now and at every boot:

           sysctl -w vm.swappiness=10
           echo 'vm.swappiness = 10' > /etc/sysctl.d/90-swap.conf
           sysctl --system

       Find all settings about IPv6:

           sysctl -a | grep ipv6

FILES
       /etc/sysctl.d/*.conf
              Your settings, applied at boot (files sorted by name).

       /etc/sysctl.conf
              Read last; on RHEL 9 it only holds comments.

       /usr/lib/sysctl.d/*.conf
              Defaults from packages. Do not edit them; override them in
              /etc/sysctl.d with a file of the same name or a later one.

SEE ALSO
       sysctl.d(5), tuned-adm(8), proc(5)
